
<% content_for :title, "Chronic Pain Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-chronic-pain">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / CHRONIC PAIN
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>IT HURTS.</span>
        <span>YOU WANT</span>
        <span>A BREAK.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR PAIN FLARES
        &amp; HARD BODY DAYS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / PAIN & DRINKING -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / PAIN &amp; DRINKING
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHAT YOU WANT<br>
        RELIEF FROM.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Chronic pain is exhausting in ways that
          go beyond the physical sensation.
          Your back hurts, your legs burn,
          your feet go numb, and ordinary
          activities become complicated.
          Standing, walking, bending,
          and even finding a comfortable
          position can take more effort
          than they should.
        </p>

        <p>
          You aren't only dealing with pain.
          You're dealing with everything
          the pain takes away. Independence,
          mobility, spontaneity, sleep,
          and the ability to do things
          without planning around
          your limitations.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol has offered you relief before.
          It changed how your body felt,
          quieted some of the frustration,
          and made difficult moments
          temporarily more tolerable.
          You remember that relief
          because you experienced it.
        </p>

        <p>
          But alcohol didn't treat the
          underlying problem. It impaired
          your judgment, created additional
          risks, and contributed to
          consequences that lasted
          much longer than the relief.
          You got a DUI because of it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to deny that
          drinking once made you feel better.
          You do have to remember
          what happened afterward.
          Your history includes trying
          to manage severe pain with
          alcohol and ending up
          with a DUI.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            Your pain deserves treatment.
            Your desire for relief makes sense.
            Alcohol doesn't have to be
            part of the solution.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / RELAPSE WARNING SIGNS -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / YOUR RELAPSE WARNING SIGNS
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHEN PAIN<br>
        STARTS BARGAINING.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- PHYSICAL WARNING SIGNS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THE BODY PART</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention when your usual
            pain increases, your sciatica
            becomes more intense, or
            numbness makes ordinary
            movement more difficult.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long periods of discomfort,
            poor sleep, and difficulty
            finding relief can wear
            down your patience.
            When everything hurts,
            even small inconveniences
            can feel unbearable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice when you start
            dreading another day
            of the same symptoms
            or feeling desperate
            for anything that might
            change how your body feels.
          </p>

          <p>
            That's when alcohol can
            start looking less like
            a bad decision and more
            like an available solution.
            Recognize the shift
            before you act on it.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- EMOTIONAL WARNING SIGNS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THE EMOTIONAL PART</h3>

          <p>
            Pain can make you angry,
            impatient, discouraged,
            bored, and resentful.
            You may get tired of
            asking for help, changing
            plans, or being unable
            to do things that
            used to be easy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for thoughts like
            "I can't take this anymore,"
            "Nothing is helping,"
            or "I just want one
            night without feeling this."
          </p>

          <p>
            These thoughts don't
            automatically mean you
            want to drink. But if
            you start remembering
            alcohol as the fastest
            way to get comfortable,
            take that seriously.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pain, isolation, and
            disrupted sleep can also
            affect your mood.
            With bipolar disorder,
            changes in energy,
            irritability, sleep,
            or impulsivity deserve
            additional attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You're allowed to hate
              being in pain. You don't
              have to turn that
              frustration into
              another problem.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / FLARE-DAY PLAN -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / YOUR FLARE-DAY PLAN
      </p>

      <h2>
        WORK WITH<br>
        TODAY'S BODY.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- PAIN MANAGEMENT -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR ESTABLISHED PAIN PLAN</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the treatments
            and instructions your doctor has already recommended.
            Follow prescribed medication
            directions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjust your position, take
            breaks, and use any heat,
            cold, or gentle movement
            your doc has confirmed
            is appropriate for your back.
            Don't force activity through
            worsening symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your usual treatment
            isn't controlling the pain,
            contact your medical team.
            You don't have to wait
            until you're desperate
            before asking whether
            your plan needs adjusting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't improvise by combining
            alcohol with medications
            or changing doses on
            your own.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Your pain plan should
              come from your doc, not the liquor store.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- EXPECTATIONS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHAT CAN WAIT</h3>

          <p>
            Some days your body has
            less capacity than others.
            Adjust your expectations
            instead of repeatedly
            pushing past your limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Housework can wait.
            Nonessential errands
            can wait. Creative
            projects can wait.
            You don't need to
            prove your productivity
            while you're hurting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Break necessary tasks
            into smaller pieces.
            Sit when possible.
            Take breaks before
            you've exhausted yourself.
            Cancel or reschedule
            things when you need to.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can still enjoy
            your day without
            accomplishing much.
            Watch a movie, listen
            to music, read,
            or work on something
            creative in a position
            that doesn't aggravate
            your symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rest is not an
            accomplishment you
            have to earn.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- ASKING FOR HELP -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>SUPPORT YOU CAN ASK FOR</h3>

          <p>
            You don't have to
            do everything yourself
            just because you're
            accustomed to being
            independent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask someone to help
            with groceries, rides
            to appointments, carrying
            heavy items, or tasks
            that require bending
            and standing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need company,
            ask for that too.
            Being stuck at home
            in pain can become
            isolating, and sometimes
            a conversation makes
            the day more tolerable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be specific about
            what you need.
            "Can you pick up
            my prescription?"
            is easier to answer
            than expecting someone
            to figure out how
            to help.
          </p>

          <p>
            Needing assistance
            doesn't mean you've
            lost your independence.
            It means you're
            working with your
            current limitations.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- DRINKING URGE -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR DRINKING-URGE PLAN</h3>

          <p>
            When pain starts
            making alcohol
            look appealing,
            recognize what's
            happening before
            the bargaining
            goes any further.
          </p>

          <p>
            You want relief.
            That's understandable.
            But remember your
            actual history
            with drinking
            to manage pain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Play the tape forward.
            Alcohol might
            temporarily change
            your experience,
            but it won't resolve
            your spinal condition.
            It can impair judgment
            and complicate treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't buy alcohol,
            browse delivery options,
            or make plans around
            drinking. Give yourself
            ten minutes to choose
            a different action.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow your pain plan,
            find a tolerable resting
            position, contact your
            docs if needed,
            and tell someone in
            your recovery circle
            that you're struggling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              You can need relief
              immediately without
              choosing a solution
              that hurts you later.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / WHEN THE PLAN NEEDS HELP -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / WHEN THE PLAN NEEDS HELP
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHO DO<br>
        YOU CONTACT?
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- RECOVERY SUPPORT -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR RECOVERY SUPPORT</h3>

          <p>
            If pain is making you
            seriously consider
            drinking, tell someone
            before you act.
          </p>


          <p>
            You can say:
            "My pain is really bad
            today, and I'm starting
            to think about drinking
            to make it stop.
            Can you talk with me?"
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need to pretend
            everything is fine
            or wait until the
            craving becomes overwhelming.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your first contact
            is unavailable, try
            someone else, attend
            a
            meeting, or reach
            out to your
            therapist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asking for help
            before drinking
            is a decision
            you can make
            while you're
            still in pain.
          </p>

        </div>


        </div>

      </div>
<!-- BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS -->
<nav class="index-home-nav" aria-label="Return to relapse plans">
  <%= link_to "← BACK TO RELAPSE PLANS",
      toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
      class: "index-home-button" %>
</nav>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
